{"PubTator3": [{"_id": "32272925|PMC7146901", "id": "7146901", "infons": {}, "passages": [{"infons": {"name_3": "surname:Lee;given-names:Joseph H.", "name_2": "surname:Druley;given-names:Todd", "name_1": "surname:Wojczynski;given-names:Mary K.", "name_0": "surname:Carlson;given-names:Paige", "year": "2020", "article-id_publisher-id": "710", "article-id_doi": "10.1186/s12920-020-0710-5", "title": "Keywords", "type": "front", "elocation-id": "61", "volume": "13", "license": "Open AccessThis article is licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License, which permits use, sharing, adaptation, distribution and reproduction in any medium or format, as long as you give appropriate credit to the original author(s) and the source, provide a link to the Creative Commons licence, and indicate if changes were made. The images or other third party material in this article are included in the article's Creative Commons licence, unless indicated otherwise in a credit line to the material. If material is not included in the article's Creative Commons licence and your intended use is not permitted by statutory regulation or exceeds the permitted use, you will need to obtain permission directly from the copyright holder. To view a copy of this licence, visit http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/. The Creative Commons Public Domain Dedication waiver (http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/) applies to the data made available in this article, unless otherwise stated in a credit line to the data.", "section_type": "TITLE", "kwd": "Incidental genetic findings Long lived families Population studies Pathogenic variants", "name_5": "surname:Thyagarajan;given-names:Bharat", "name_4": "surname:Zmuda;given-names:Joseph M.", "article-id_pmc": "7146901", "article-id_pmid": "32272925"}, "offset": 0, "text": "Prevalence of clinically actionable disease variants in exceptionally long-lived families", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "ABSTRACT", "type": "abstract_title_1"}, "offset": 90, "text": "Background", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "ABSTRACT", "type": "abstract"}, "offset": 101, "text": "Phenotypic expression of pathogenic variants in individuals with no family history of inherited disorders remains unclear.", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21699", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D030342", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D030342"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D030342", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Genetic Diseases Inborn", "accession": "@DISEASE_Genetic_Diseases_Inborn"}, "text": "inherited disorders", "locations": [{"offset": 187, "length": 19}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "ABSTRACT", "type": "abstract_title_1"}, "offset": 224, "text": "Methods", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "ABSTRACT", "type": "abstract"}, "offset": 232, "text": "We evaluated the prevalence of pathogenic variants in 25 genes associated with Mendelian-inherited disorders in 3015 participants from 485 families in the Long Life Family Study (LLFS). Boot-strapping and Fisher's exact test were used to determine whether allele frequencies in LLFS were significantly different from the allele frequencies reported in publicly available genomic databases.", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21700", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D030342", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D030342"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D030342", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Genetic Diseases Inborn", "accession": "@DISEASE_Genetic_Diseases_Inborn"}, "text": "Mendelian-inherited disorders", "locations": [{"offset": 311, "length": 29}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "ABSTRACT", "type": "abstract_title_1"}, "offset": 624, "text": "Results", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "ABSTRACT", "type": "abstract"}, "offset": 632, "text": "The proportions of pathogenic autosomal dominant mutation carriers in BRCA1 and SDHC in LLFS study participants were similar to those reported in publicly available genomic databases (0.03% vs. 0.0008%, p = 1 for BRCA1, and 0.08% vs. 0.003%, p = 0.05 for SDHC). The frequency of carriers of pathogenic autosomal recessive variants in CPT2, ACADM, SUMF1, WRN, ATM, and ACADVL were also similar in LLFS as compared to those reported in genomic databases. The lack of clinical disease among LLFS participants with well-established pathogenic variants in BRCA1 and SDHC suggests that penetrance of pathogenic variants may be different in long lived families.", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21701", "infons": {"identifier": "672", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "5276", "valid": true, "normalized": [672], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 672, "biotype": "gene", "name": "BRCA1", "accession": "@GENE_BRCA1"}, "text": "BRCA1", "locations": [{"offset": 702, "length": 5}]}, {"id": "21702", "infons": {"identifier": "6391", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "2256", "valid": true, "normalized": [6391], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 6391, "biotype": "gene", "name": "SDHC", "accession": "@GENE_SDHC"}, "text": "SDHC", "locations": [{"offset": 712, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21703", "infons": {"identifier": "672", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "5276", "valid": true, "normalized": [672], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 672, "biotype": "gene", "name": "BRCA1", "accession": "@GENE_BRCA1"}, "text": "BRCA1", "locations": [{"offset": 845, "length": 5}]}, {"id": "21704", "infons": {"identifier": "6391", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "2256", "valid": true, "normalized": [6391], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 6391, "biotype": "gene", "name": "SDHC", "accession": "@GENE_SDHC"}, "text": "SDHC", "locations": [{"offset": 887, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21705", "infons": {"identifier": "1376", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "77", "valid": true, "normalized": [1376], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 1376, "biotype": "gene", "name": "CPT2", "accession": "@GENE_CPT2"}, "text": "CPT2", "locations": [{"offset": 966, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21706", "infons": {"identifier": "34", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "3", "valid": true, "normalized": [34], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 34, "biotype": "gene", "name": "ACADM", "accession": "@GENE_ACADM"}, "text": "ACADM", "locations": [{"offset": 972, "length": 5}]}, {"id": "21707", "infons": {"identifier": "285362", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "16268", "valid": true, "normalized": [285362], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 285362, "biotype": "gene", "name": "SUMF1", "accession": "@GENE_SUMF1"}, "text": "SUMF1", "locations": [{"offset": 979, "length": 5}]}, {"id": "21708", "infons": {"identifier": "7486", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "6659", "valid": true, "normalized": [7486], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 7486, "biotype": "gene", "name": "WRN", "accession": "@GENE_WRN"}, "text": "WRN", "locations": [{"offset": 986, "length": 3}]}, {"id": "21709", "infons": {"identifier": "472", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "30952", "valid": true, "normalized": [472], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 472, "biotype": "gene", "name": "ATM", "accession": "@GENE_ATM"}, "text": "ATM", "locations": [{"offset": 991, "length": 3}]}, {"id": "21710", "infons": {"identifier": "37", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "5", "valid": true, "normalized": [37], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 37, "biotype": "gene", "name": "ACADVL", "accession": "@GENE_ACADVL"}, "text": "ACADVL", "locations": [{"offset": 1000, "length": 6}]}, {"id": "21711", "infons": {"identifier": "672", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "5276", "valid": true, "normalized": [672], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 672, "biotype": "gene", "name": "BRCA1", "accession": "@GENE_BRCA1"}, "text": "BRCA1", "locations": [{"offset": 1183, "length": 5}]}, {"id": "21712", "infons": {"identifier": "6391", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "2256", "valid": true, "normalized": [6391], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 6391, "biotype": "gene", "name": "SDHC", "accession": "@GENE_SDHC"}, "text": "SDHC", "locations": [{"offset": 1193, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21713", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 1120, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21714", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 720, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21715", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 1028, "length": 4}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "ABSTRACT", "type": "abstract_title_1"}, "offset": 1295, "text": "Conclusion", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "ABSTRACT", "type": "abstract"}, "offset": 1306, "text": "Further research is needed to better understand the penetrance of pathogenic variants before expanding large scale genomic testing to asymptomatic individuals.", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "INTRO", "type": "title_1"}, "offset": 1466, "text": "Introduction", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "INTRO", "type": "paragraph"}, "offset": 1479, "text": "Next generation sequencing (NGS) has revolutionized the genetic diagnosis for several diseases such as hearing loss, vision loss, cardiovascular disorders, and neurodegenerative disorders through testing of single genes, targeted gene panels, and whole exome sequencing. Large scale sequencing efforts are increasingly being used in several research studies that include participants in the general population without a strong family history or with subtle clinical presentations. Large scale sequencing effort such as the 1000 Genomes Project and 6500 Exome Sequencing Project (ESP) have demonstrated that phenotypically healthy individuals harbor variants previously classified as pathogenic, calling into question the pathogenicity of these variants. This misclassification may be due to initial classification of these variants being based on studies with limited sample size or incomplete penetrance of specific pathogenic variants. The American College of Medical Genetics and Genomics (ACMG) has published a specific list of medically actionable genes known to cause autosomal dominant conditions in order to provide guidance on return of secondary genetic findings to patients and/or their care-givers when undergoing whole genome and whole exome sequencing in the context of clinical sequencing. However, the variable penetrance of rare variants, particularly in the absence of relevant family history, remains a major limitation in expanding large scale sequencing efforts beyond the narrowly defined clinical settings into the general population.", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21716", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:C566739", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["C566739"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "C566739", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Multiple Pterygium Syndrome Autosomal Dominant", "accession": "@DISEASE_Multiple_Pterygium_Syndrome_Autosomal_Dominant"}, "text": "autosomal dominant conditions", "locations": [{"offset": 2553, "length": 29}]}, {"id": "21717", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D019636", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D019636"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D019636", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Neurodegenerative Diseases", "accession": "@DISEASE_Neurodegenerative_Diseases"}, "text": "neurodegenerative disorders", "locations": [{"offset": 1639, "length": 27}]}, {"id": "21718", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D014786", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D014786"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D014786", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Vision Disorders", "accession": "@DISEASE_Vision_Disorders"}, "text": "vision loss", "locations": [{"offset": 1596, "length": 11}]}, {"id": "21719", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D002318", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D002318"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D002318", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Cardiovascular Diseases", "accession": "@DISEASE_Cardiovascular_Diseases"}, "text": "cardiovascular disorders", "locations": [{"offset": 1609, "length": 24}]}, {"id": "21720", "infons": {"identifier": "9606", "type": "Species", "valid": true, "normalized": [9606], "database": "ncbi_taxonomy", "normalized_id": 9606, "biotype": "species", "name": "9606", "accession": null}, "text": "patients", "locations": [{"offset": 2655, "length": 8}]}, {"id": "21721", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D034381", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D034381"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D034381", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Hearing Loss", "accession": "@DISEASE_Hearing_Loss"}, "text": "hearing loss", "locations": [{"offset": 1582, "length": 12}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "INTRO", "type": "paragraph"}, "offset": 3037, "text": "Studies on centenarians are particularly informative in this regard as variants previously classified as pathogenic but found in these individuals are less likely to be a pathogenic variant that causes highly penetrant monogenic Mendelian diseases that typically have an early onset of disease. A previous study on 44 Ashkenazi Jewish centenarians who underwent whole genome sequencing identified over 130 pathogenic or likely pathogenic variants present in the centenarians' genomes; the authors concluded that mutations previously classified as pathogenic might not necessarily preclude a long life.", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21722", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D030342", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D030342"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D030342", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Genetic Diseases Inborn", "accession": "@DISEASE_Genetic_Diseases_Inborn"}, "text": "Mendelian diseases", "locations": [{"offset": 3266, "length": 18}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "INTRO", "type": "paragraph"}, "offset": 3641, "text": "Since this study was limited to 44 centenarians of Ashkenazi Jewish descent, we evaluated the prevalence of established pathogenic and likely pathogenic variants in long-lived families of broad European descent in Long Life Family Study (LLFS), a family-based cohort study designed to evaluate genetic and environmental factors associated with exceptional survival to extend the generalizability of the previous study findings to a broader population. In addition, the family-based design of LLFS also allowed us to evaluate segregation patterns of pathogenic mutations within families. This study analyzed sequences from 25 loci associated with hereditary disorders and compared the prevalence of pathogenic variants in LLFS participants vs. a general population of predominantly European ancestry found in publicly available genomic databases of germline variants.", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21723", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D009386", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D009386"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D009386", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Neoplastic Syndromes Hereditary", "accession": "@DISEASE_Neoplastic_Syndromes_Hereditary"}, "text": "hereditary disorders", "locations": [{"offset": 4287, "length": 20}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "METHODS", "type": "title_1"}, "offset": 4508, "text": "Methods", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "METHODS", "type": "title_2"}, "offset": 4516, "text": "Study population", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "METHODS", "type": "paragraph"}, "offset": 4533, "text": "LLFS enrolled long-lived probands and their siblings (n = 1445; baseline age: 91 +- 8 years), their offspring (n = 2346; baseline age: 61 +- 8 years) and spousal controls (n = 785; baseline age 62 +- 8 years) from three U.S. field centers (New York City, Boston, and Pittsburgh) and one Danish field center. The study design and selection criteria have been described previously. Included in this analysis were 3015 participants from 485 families (including spousal controls) in three US field centers. Danish participants were excluded as Danish participants did not have appropriate consent for participation in this genetic study. Participants of non-European ancestry were also excluded to minimize issues related to variant classification in non-Caucasian populations.", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21724", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 4533, "length": 4}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "METHODS", "type": "title_2"}, "offset": 5337, "text": "Sequencing of variants", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "METHODS", "type": "paragraph"}, "offset": 5360, "text": "The LLFS participants had 464 genes sequenced that were selected collectively by the LLFS investigators due to their published association with age-related phenotypes. Of these 464 genes, 25 genes associated with Mendelian forms of metabolic defects, familial cardiovascular disorders, familial cancer predispositions, and familial neurodegenerative disorders (Supplementary Table 1) were included for further analysis in this study. This included seven genes on the ACMG list of secondary genetic findings and the remaining 18 genes associated with autosomal recessive conditions were included to evaluate whether rates carrier status for Mendelian diseases were different in LLFS families as compared to the general population.", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21725", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D009369", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D009369"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D009369", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Neoplasms", "accession": "@DISEASE_Neoplasms"}, "text": "cancer", "locations": [{"offset": 5655, "length": 6}]}, {"id": "21726", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 5445, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21727", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 6037, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21728", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D019636", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D019636"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D019636", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Neurodegenerative Diseases", "accession": "@DISEASE_Neurodegenerative_Diseases"}, "text": "neurodegenerative disorders", "locations": [{"offset": 5692, "length": 27}]}, {"id": "21729", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D002318", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D002318"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D002318", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Cardiovascular Diseases", "accession": "@DISEASE_Cardiovascular_Diseases"}, "text": "cardiovascular disorders", "locations": [{"offset": 5620, "length": 24}]}, {"id": "21730", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D030342", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D030342"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D030342", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Genetic Diseases Inborn", "accession": "@DISEASE_Genetic_Diseases_Inborn"}, "text": "Mendelian diseases", "locations": [{"offset": 6000, "length": 18}]}, {"id": "21731", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 5364, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21732", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D008659", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D008659"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D008659", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Metabolic Diseases", "accession": "@DISEASE_Metabolic_Diseases"}, "text": "metabolic defects", "locations": [{"offset": 5592, "length": 17}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "METHODS", "type": "title_2"}, "offset": 6091, "text": "Variant classification", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "METHODS", "type": "paragraph"}, "offset": 6114, "text": "We used ANNOVAR to annotate the genetic variants. The prevalence of the annotated variants was found using public genome variant databases such as gnomAD v2, which consists of exome and genome data from 141,456 individuals sequenced as part of various disease-specific and population genetic studies. Approximately 55% of individuals included in gnomAD v2 were of European ancestry and 46% of individuals were women. All variants with an allele frequency < 0.5% in the general population were further evaluated manually and classified into five categories using the ACMG criteria: pathogenic, likely pathogenic, uncertain significance, likely benign, and benign. In addition to public genome variant databases such as those listed above, we used publicly available databases of clinically relevant genetic variants such as ClinVar to determine if the rare variants had been previously classified as pathogenic or likely pathogenic by other clinical laboratories. Variants not previously classified in ClinVar were evaluated using locus/disease specific databases (LOVDs). If the variant was not classified in ClinVar or LOVDs, we conducted a literature search in PubMed using common nomenclatures, such as dbSNP reference numbers, gene specific mutations, and chromosome position, to identify peer-reviewed publications that had identified the mutations.", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21733", "infons": {"identifier": "9606", "type": "Species", "valid": true, "normalized": [9606], "database": "ncbi_taxonomy", "normalized_id": 9606, "biotype": "species", "name": "9606", "accession": null}, "text": "women", "locations": [{"offset": 6524, "length": 5}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "METHODS", "type": "paragraph"}, "offset": 7473, "text": "In silico predictions were obtained using MutationTaster, GERP, phyloP100way, LRT, SIFT, and PolyPhen-2. If three or more of these programs listed the variant as pathogenic or conserved, then the criterion for PP1 was met. We used GERP scores of greater than 3.0 to be pathogenic. We utilized an online genetic interpretation tool from the University of Maryland School of Medicine to assist with and standardize variant classification using the ACMG criteria. All variants classified as pathogenic/likely pathogenic were manually reviewed and confirmed by a molecular and genetic pathologist (BT).", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21734", "infons": {"identifier": "5540", "type": "Gene", "ncbi_homologene": "116086", "valid": true, "normalized": [5540], "database": "ncbi_gene", "normalized_id": 5540, "biotype": "gene", "name": "NPY4R", "accession": "@GENE_NPY4R"}, "text": "PP1", "locations": [{"offset": 7683, "length": 3}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "METHODS", "type": "title_2"}, "offset": 8072, "text": "Statistical analysis", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "METHODS", "type": "paragraph"}, "offset": 8093, "text": "We used Fisher's exact test to compare the allele frequencies in LLFS and the general population of European descent in the gnomAD database. Since LLFS is a family-based study, the observed allele frequencies were skewed by the prevalence of genetic variants within individual families. To obtain an estimate of the population frequency of variants in LLFS, we estimated the allele frequency in LLFS by randomly selecting one family member from the LLFS families, repeating the procedure 1000 times to determine an average estimate of the allele frequency in the LLFS population.", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21735", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 8445, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21736", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 8656, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21737", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 8240, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21738", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 8488, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21739", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 8542, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21740", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 8158, "length": 4}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "RESULTS", "type": "title_1"}, "offset": 8675, "text": "Results", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "RESULTS", "type": "paragraph"}, "offset": 8683, "text": "The average age of LLFS participants in this study was 71 +- 16 years, and women comprised 55% of all participants. Among 1372 variants identified in the 25 genes, 283 nonsynonymous and stop-gain variants were identified for further review after excluding common variants (variants present in > 0.5% of the general population). Of the 283 variants, seven (2.4%) were stop-gain variants and 276 (97.6%) were nonsynonymous variants. Nine variants (3.2%) were classified as likely pathogenic or pathogenic, 241 variants (85.1%) were classified as variants of uncertain significance (VUS), and the remaining variants were classified as likely benign (11.7%).", "sentences": [], "annotations": [{"id": "21741", "infons": {"identifier": "MESH:D000094024", "type": "Disease", "valid": true, "normalized": ["D000094024"], "database": "ncbi_mesh", "normalized_id": "D000094024", "biotype": "disease", "name": "Post-Acute COVID-19 Syndrome", "accession": "@DISEASE_Post_Acute_COVID_19_Syndrome"}, "text": "LLFS", "locations": [{"offset": 8702, "length": 4}]}, {"id": "21742", "infons": {"identifier": "9606", "type": "Species", "valid": true, "normalized": [9606], "database": "ncbi_taxonomy", "normalized_id": 9606, "biotype": "species", "name": "9606", "accession": null}, "text": "women", "locations": [{"offset": 8758, "length": 5}]}], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "TABLE", "file": "Tab1.xml", "id": "Tab1", "type": "table_caption"}, "offset": 9346, "text": "Pathogenic and likely pathogenic classified variants identified in Long Life Family Study", "sentences": [], "annotations": [], "relations": []}, {"infons": {"section_type": "TABLE", "file": "Tab1.xml", "xml": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"UTF-8\"?> <table frame=\"hsides\" rules=\"groups\"><thead><tr><th>SNP ID<sup><bold>*</bold></sup></th><th>Gene</th><th>Amino Acid Change</th><th>Random LLFS Sampling Frequency</th><th>Population Frequency</th><th><bold><italic>p</italic></bol